`c_secret_emit_guard(` is **absent** from `c_emit_print.oo`.
`id == "eprintln"` is **absent** from `c_emit_ident.oo`.

========================================================================
MECHANISM (why eprintln sees it and stmt println does not)
========================================================================

Stmt rail (`c_emit_print.oo` L19–30):

```
while sp < n {
    sk = token
    if COMMA || RPAREN || SEMI { break }   # no depth
    if IDENT && __sec__sn == "1" { refuse }
    sp++
}
# then c_emit_expr_c — emit only; no second refuse
```

`println(("x") + tok)` tokens after LPAREN:
`LPAREN STRING RPAREN PLUS IDENT(tok) RPAREN`

Scan hits the **grouping** `RPAREN` and stops. Never looks at `tok`.
`c_emit_expr_c` then lowers the full `(("x") + tok)` and prints it.

Check rail (`c_emit_secret.oo` L175–193) for **both** names:

```
optional skip LPAREN
while tokens:
    if RPAREN { stop }          # no depth
    if IDENT { refuse_resolved }
```

Same first-`)` stop. `check` rc=0 `OK` on the hole shapes.

Free-call rail (`c_emit_lower.oo` L90 + guard L213–226):

```
args = joined C text   # e.g. oo_str_concat((oo_str_lit("x")), tok)
c_secret_emit_guard(..., "eprintln", ..., args)
c_guard_scan_idents  # string-aware; still slices bare ident tok
```

That is the only rail that still sees `tok` after a nested `)`.

Expr-position `let x = println(tok)` is **not** the stmt rail: let
uses `c_emit_expr_c` → guard. Alias `let f = println; f(tok)` is
front-door `resolve_alias`. Those are **not** counterexamples to this
OWN.

